From 107feea1cb06493295558643f3046dd5d6bbbcaf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:25:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] JavaScriptCore: the 68k template JIT sooner, and once more
 when its caches fill

Functions and loops go to the template JIT after about a fifth of the
interpreter's run they needed before: thresholdForJITAfterWarmUp is 100 on
the 68k (500 elsewhere), so a function is compiled on about its seventh
call (op_enter counts 5, op_ret 10) and a loop at its 100th iteration. The
template JIT copies the interpreter's handlers, so its compile is cheap
next to the interpreting it saves (V8's Sparkplug makes the same trade).

Compiling sooner means more sites whose inline caches are still empty
when the code is written, and those get the plain template instead of
fast code: get_by_id and put_by_id with no structure yet, global
variables and scopes not yet resolved, calls that have not called
anything (no inlining, no built-in in line). The compile now notes those
cache words. At the end of each collection, if one has filled, the code
is replaced once: the CodeBlock goes back to the interpreter, which
compiles it again after m68kTemplateJITRecompileThreshold (100) more.
Replaced code stays allocated, with its structure tables still cleared of
dead structures, until the CodeBlock dies, since a frame or a caller can
still be running it; it leaves through redispatch to the current code.
JSC_useM68KTemplateJITRecompile=0 turns the second compile off.

JSC_reportM68KTemplateJITStatistics=1 prints at exit what was compiled
(compiles, second compiles, bytes) and how often the interpreter's
get_by_id, put_by_id, get_by_val and put_by_val missed, for the
interpreter cache work that follows.

